## The problem is not components

Design systems are often equated with a set of ready-made components. Yet the problem I run into most is not that — it is **small decisions repeated over and over**: which gray to use for secondary text, how far apart sections should be, and how things look in dark mode.

If those decisions live in one place, components follow naturally.

## Start with tokens

```css
:root {
  --bg: #fafaf9;
  --surface: #ffffff;
  --ink: #18181b;
  --muted: #63636b;
  --line: #e4e4e7;
  --accent: #4f46e5;
}

.dark {
  --bg: #0b0b0d;
  --surface: #121215;
  --ink: #f4f4f5;
  --muted: #a1a1aa;
  --line: #27272a;
  --accent: #a5b4fc;
}
```

With Tailwind v4, these tokens map directly to utilities through `@theme inline`, so we keep writing `bg-surface` and `text-muted` instead of raw colors.

## Limit the palette

The rule that helped me most: **one accent color, everything else neutral**. If something needs to stand out, use size, weight, or spacing — not a new color.

The side effect is pleasant: dark mode is easier because only one variable changes, and the whole thing feels calm.

## Consistent spacing

I use a single spacing scale and avoid arbitrary numbers. Large section gaps are always `py-20` or `py-24`, gaps inside cards are always small multiples. That consistency is felt more than any animation.

## When do you need a library?

If you need complex accessibility — dialogs, comboboxes, date pickers — use a library. But for layout, typography, and cards, a few tokens are more than enough.
